Productive Memory Distortion: Mathematical Foundations

ouadi Maakoul 

AI: chatGpt, Deepseek, Meta AI , KIMI .

Abstract

This dissertation develops a mathematical theory of memory as a resource for counterfactual simulation. Given finite episodic experience generated under confounding, what should an agent retain, transform, or discard so that memory supports intervention rather than mere replay? Memory is modeled as a representation inducing a simulation channel P_{\text{sim}}(\hat Y \mid do(X=x), \widetilde M), and utility is defined by interventional cross-entropy, U_f(\widetilde M)=H(Y)-\mathrm{CE}_{\text{sim}}(\widetilde M). Under spurious reliance, exact memory is shown to incur a strictly positive spurious liability \delta(M)>0: its maximum-likelihood simulator encodes contextual associations that are observationally predictive but interventionally non-causal. The central invariance criterion identifies zero spurious error with measurability of the simulation channel with respect to the counterfactual-invariant \sigma-algebra \mathcal F_{X_Z^\perp}. Under falsity exposure violation, no distribution-preserving retention of the same episodes recovers this invariance: deletion removes data but cannot create the environmental diversity whose absence constitutes the failure. A separation theorem then distinguishes transformation from abstraction. Quotient-only abstractions that must collapse a vertex carrying both spurious and invariant edges pay a strictly positive transport cost \Delta_{\text{geo}}>0, which under a rate–transport link translates into a strictly positive rate penalty. Non-homomorphic distortions rewrite spurious edges without collapsing invariant structure. Assembling these results yields a conditional existence theorem for Productive Memory Distortion: under an explicit epistemic prior over which edges are spurious, a rate constraint, FEC violation, and a local distortion geometry, there exists D^\ast such that the distorted memory strictly dominates exact memory, distribution-preserving retention, and quotient-only abstraction, yielding positive absolute and separation gains. The theory does not assume its prior is correct. Its empirical content is a falsification protocol: the prior must predict which associations are spurious across environments, and the separation gain must vanish when FEC holds, interventional volatility vanishes, or abstraction is already non-homomorphic. The dissertation thereby gives a first-principles account of productive forgetting: optimal memory for counterfactual simulation is the minimal non-homomorphic causal distortion that achieves counterfactual-invariant measurability at a fixed rate, conditional on a falsifiable hypothesis about the invariant mechanism.

Keywords: productive memory distortion, counterfactual simulation, causal invariance, spurious liability, rate–distortion, abstraction, forgetting, falsifiability.

