1. Mathematical Preliminaries

1.1 Kernels, Probability, and Total Variation

Let (\Omega, \mathcal B, \mathbb P) be a probability space. For measurable spaces (\mathcal X, \mathcal B_{\mathcal X}) and (\mathcal Y, \mathcal B_{\mathcal Y}), a Markov kernel from \mathcal X to \mathcal Y is a map K: \mathcal X \times \mathcal B_{\mathcal Y} \to [0,1] such that x \mapsto K(x, B) is measurable for each B \in \mathcal B_{\mathcal Y} and B \mapsto K(x, B) is a probability measure for each x \in \mathcal X. We write K: \mathcal X \rightsquigarrow \mathcal Y.

Composition: if K: \mathcal X \rightsquigarrow \mathcal Y and L: \mathcal Y \rightsquigarrow \mathcal Z, then LK: \mathcal X \rightsquigarrow \mathcal Z is (LK)(x, C) = \int_{\mathcal Y} L(y, C) K(x, dy).

Definition 1.1 (Total variation). For two probability measures \mu, \nu on (\mathcal X, \mathcal B_{\mathcal X}),

\|\mu - \nu\|_{\mathrm{TV}} = \sup_{A \in \mathcal B_{\mathcal X}} |\mu(A) - \nu(A)| = \frac12 \int |d\mu - d\nu|.

For two kernels K, L: \mathcal X \rightsquigarrow \mathcal Y,

\|K - L\|_{\mathrm{TV}} = \sup_{x \in \mathcal X} \|K(x, \cdot) - L(x, \cdot)\|_{\mathrm{TV}}.

Proposition 1.2 (Continuity of cross-entropy under TV). Let P, Q be probability measures on a finite set \mathcal Y with P(y) > 0 for all y. Then

|\mathbb E_P[-\log Q] - \mathbb E_P[-\log Q']| \le \frac{2\|Q - Q'\|_{\mathrm{TV}}}{\min_y Q'(y)}.

In particular, Q \mapsto \mathbb E_P[-\log Q] is continuous in TV on the set of measures bounded below by a positive constant.

Proof. For each y, |\log Q(y) - \log Q'(y)| \le |Q(y) - Q'(y)| / \min_y Q'(y) by the mean value theorem. Summing against P(y) \le 1 gives the bound. \square

1.2 Structural Causal Models and Interventions

Definition 1.3 (SCM). A structural causal model is a tuple W = (U, V, F) where U are exogenous variables, V = (V_1, \dots, V_k) are endogenous variables, and F = (f_1, \dots, f_k) are structural equations V_i = f_i(\mathrm{pa}(V_i), U_i) with \mathrm{pa}(V_i) \subseteq V \cup U.

In this work we specialize to W = (C, S, Y) with

C = f_C(U_C), \qquad S = f_S(C, U_S), \qquad Y = f_Y(C, S, U_Y),

so that C are invariant causal factors, S are spurious/contextual factors, Y is the target. The action is X = (X_C, X_S) with X_C acting on C and X_S on S.

Definition 1.4 (Intervention). For Z \subseteq \{C, S, Y\} and a value z, the do-operator do(Z = z) replaces the structural equations for Z with constants z, yielding the intervened SCM W_{do(Z=z)}. The interventional distribution is P(\cdot \mid do(Z=z)).

Definition 1.5 (Counterfactual). For an intervention do(Z=z), the counterfactual X(z) of a variable X is its value in W_{do(Z=z)} under the same exogenous U.

1.3 Counterfactual Invariance

Definition 1.6 (Counterfactual-invariant \sigma-algebra). For Z \subseteq S and the family of interventions \{do(Z = z)\}_z,

\mathcal F_{X_Z^\perp} = \sigma\bigl(\{X(z)\}_z\bigr) \cap \sigma(X).

This is the \sigma-algebra of events that are measurable with respect to the counterfactual action family and the factual action, i.e., the events that do not depend on Z.

Theorem 1.7 (Veitch et al., 2021). A kernel P_{\text{sim}}(\cdot \mid do(X), \widetilde M) satisfies

P_{\text{sim}}(\hat Y \mid do(Z = z), \widetilde M) = P_{\text{sim}}(\hat Y \mid \widetilde M) \quad \forall Z \subseteq S,\ \forall z

if and only if P_{\text{sim}} is \mathcal F_{X_Z^\perp}-measurable.

1.4 Optimal Transport and Wasserstein Distance

Definition 1.8 (Coupling). For probability measures \mu, \nu on a Polish space (\mathcal E, d), a coupling is a probability measure \gamma on \mathcal E \times \mathcal E with marginals \mu, \nu. Write \Gamma(\mu, \nu) for the set of couplings.

Definition 1.9 (Wasserstein distance). For a cost c: \mathcal E \times \mathcal E \to [0, \infty),

W_c(\mu, \nu) = \inf_{\gamma \in \Gamma(\mu, \nu)} \mathbb E_{(e, \tilde e) \sim \gamma}[c(e, \tilde e)].

For c(e, \tilde e) = \|\phi(e) - \phi(\tilde e)\|^2 with \phi: \mathcal E \to \mathbb R^d, this is the squared Wasserstein-2 distance between pushforwards \phi_\# \mu, \phi_\# \nu.

Proposition 1.10 (Attainment). If \mathcal E is compact and \phi is continuous, then \Gamma(\mu, \nu) is tight and the infimum in W_c is attained.

Proof. \mathcal E compact \implies \mathcal E \times \mathcal E compact \implies the set of probability measures on \mathcal E \times \mathcal E is compact in the weak-* topology (Prokhorov). The marginal constraints are closed, so \Gamma(\mu, \nu) is compact. The map \gamma \mapsto \int c \, d\gamma is continuous (since c is continuous and bounded on compact \mathcal E \times \mathcal E). Hence the infimum is attained. \square

Proposition 1.11 (Identity). If \phi is injective, then W_c(\mu, \nu) = 0 \iff \mu = \nu. If \phi is not injective, then W_c(\mu, \nu) = 0 \iff \phi_\# \mu = \phi_\# \nu.

Proof. Wasserstein-2 distance between measures on a metric space vanishes iff the measures coincide. The pushforward claim is immediate. \square

Throughout, we assume \mathcal E compact, \phi continuous injective.

1.5 Cross-Entropy, KL Divergence, and Gibbs' Inequality

Definition 1.12 (Entropy, cross-entropy, KL). For probability measures P, Q on a finite set \mathcal Y:

H(P) = -\sum_y P(y) \log P(y), \qquad \mathrm{CE}(P, Q) = -\sum_y P(y) \log Q(y),

D_{\mathrm{KL}}(P \| Q) = \sum_y P(y) \log \frac{P(y)}{Q(y)}.

Proposition 1.13 (Gibbs). D_{\mathrm{KL}}(P \| Q) \ge 0 with equality iff P = Q. Consequently,

\mathrm{CE}(P, Q) = H(P) + D_{\mathrm{KL}}(P \| Q) \ge H(P).

1.6 Rate-Distortion Theory

Definition 1.14 (Rate-distortion function). Let \mathcal C be a class of representations, and let \mathrm{Err}: \mathcal C \to \mathbb R_{\ge 0} be a distortion functional. For D \ge 0,

R_c^{\mathcal C}(D) = \inf\{|R| : \widetilde M \in \mathcal C,\ \mathrm{Err}(\widetilde M) \le D,\ P_{\text{sim}}(\cdot \mid do(X), \widetilde M) \text{ is } \mathcal F_{X_Z^\perp}\text{-measurable}\},

where |R| is the description length in bits of the representation.

Proposition 1.15 (Monotonicity). R_c^{\mathcal C}(D) is non-increasing in D. If \mathcal C_1 \subseteq \mathcal C_2, then R_c^{\mathcal C_1}(D) \ge R_c^{\mathcal C_2}(D).

Proof. The feasible set for R_c^{\mathcal C}(D) is nested increasing in D, and \mathcal C_1 \subseteq \mathcal C_2 implies the feasible set for \mathcal C_1 is contained in that for \mathcal C_2. \square

