2. The Simulation Channel and Interventional Utility

2.1 World, Memory, and Representations

Let W = (C, S, Y) be the SCM of Section 1.2. Let

M = \{e_i\}_{i=1}^n \sim P_{\text{tr}}(C, S, Y), \qquad |M| < \infty,

be episodic memory, with P_{\text{tr}} the training distribution. Let \mathcal G_{\text{tr}} be the set of training environments, defined as the distinct contexts g = P_{\text{tr}}(C, S, Y \mid \text{context}) represented in M.

A representation is \widetilde M = f(M) with description length |R| bits.

Definition 2.1 (Distortion operator). A distortion operator \mathcal D_\theta: \mathcal M \to \mathcal M is a map from memories to memories, parameterized by \theta. The distortion level D \ge 0 is a scalar controlling the degree of distortion, so that \mathcal D_D(M) is the distorted memory at level D. We assume \mathcal D_0 = \mathrm{id}.

Definition 2.2 (Abstraction). An abstraction A is a map A: \mathcal M \to \mathcal M. We distinguish two classes:

· Quotient-only abstraction. Let G_M = (V_M, E_M) be the labeled relational graph encoded by M, with vertices corresponding to episodes (or episode clusters) and edges labeled by causal (C-type) or associational (S-type) relations. A is G_M-homomorphic if there exists a surjective node map A: V_M \to V_{A(M)} such that for every (u, v, \ell) \in E_M, (A(u), A(v), \ell) \in E_{A(M)}. No vertex deletion is permitted: A is a quotient, not a pruning. Self-loops arising from vertex identification are retained and labeled.
· Non-homomorphic distortion. \mathcal D_\theta is non-homomorphic if G_{\mathcal D_\theta(M)} \neq G_M as labeled graphs, i.e., \mathcal D_\theta can rewrite an edge (u, v, \ell) \mapsto (u, v', \ell') without identifying u and v.

2.2 The Simulation Channel

A representation \widetilde M induces a simulator, a Markov kernel

P_{\text{sim}}(\hat Y \mid do(X = x), \widetilde M),

from interventions to simulated outcomes, parameterized by \widetilde M.

Definition 2.3 (Maximum-likelihood simulator). For a memory M, the maximum-likelihood simulator is

P_{\text{sim}}(\hat Y \mid do(X = x), M) = P_M(Y \mid X = x),

the empirical conditional in M.

2.3 Interventional Utility

Definition 2.4 (Interventional utility via cross-entropy). Fix an intervention protocol \mathcal T_{cf} over X. Define

\mathrm{CE}_{\text{sim}}(\widetilde M) = \mathbb E_{x \sim \mathcal T_{cf}} \mathbb E_{y \sim P(Y \mid do(X=x))} \bigl[-\log P_{\text{sim}}(y \mid do(X=x), \widetilde M)\bigr],

and

U_f(\widetilde M) = H(Y) - \mathrm{CE}_{\text{sim}}(\widetilde M).

U_f is a functional of the simulation channel, not of the representation.

Proposition 2.5 (Normalization). If \mathcal T_{cf} = P_{\text{tr}}(X) and P_{\text{sim}}(\cdot \mid do(X=x), \widetilde M) = P_{\text{tr}}(Y \mid X = x), then U_f(\widetilde M) = I(Y; X).

Proof. In this case

\mathrm{CE}_{\text{sim}}(\widetilde M) = \mathbb E_{x \sim P_{\text{tr}}(X)} \mathbb E_{y \sim P_{\text{tr}}(Y \mid X=x)}[-\log P_{\text{tr}}(y \mid x)] = H(Y \mid X),

so U_f = H(Y) - H(Y \mid X) = I(Y; X). \square

2.4 Historical Fidelity

Definition 2.6 (Historical fidelity). Let P_M, P_{\widetilde M} be the empirical distributions over episodes. Define

D_p(M, \widetilde M) = \inf_{\gamma \in \Gamma(P_M, P_{\widetilde M})} \mathbb E_{(e, \tilde e) \sim \gamma}[\|\phi(e) - \phi(\tilde e)\|^2],

with \phi: \mathcal E \to \mathbb R^d a continuous injective feature map encoding (C, S, Y).

By Proposition 1.11, D_p(M, \widetilde M) = 0 \iff P_M = P_{\widetilde M}.

2.5 Two Separation Metrics

Definition 2.7 (Two FSG metrics). For a distortion level D,

\mathrm{FSG}_{\text{abs}}(D) := U_f(\mathcal D_D(M)) - U_f(M),

\mathrm{FSG}_{\text{sep}}(D) := U_f(\mathcal D_D(M)) - \max\{U_f(M), U_f(M_{\text{retain}}), U_f(A(M))\}.

\mathrm{FSG}_{\text{abs}} tests absolute productive distortion. \mathrm{FSG}_{\text{sep}} tests separation from rate-matched baselines.

Definition 2.8 (PMD existence). Productive Memory Distortion exists at level D iff

\mathrm{FSG}_{\text{abs}}(D) > 0 \quad \text{and} \quad \mathrm{FSG}_{\text{sep}}(D) > 0.

