3. Four Structural Lemmas

3.1 Spurious Liability (Lemma 1)

Assumption (A1) (Spurious reliance). S is predictive of Y under P_{\text{tr}} but non-causal under \mathcal T_{cf}. Moreover,

P_{\text{tr}}(Y \mid S = s) \neq P(Y)

on a set of S^\star-positive measure, where S^\star is the intervention marginal of S under \mathcal T_{cf}.

Lemma 3.1 (Spurious liability). Under (A1), let P_{\text{sim}} be the maximum-likelihood simulator on M, so that P_{\text{sim}}(\hat Y \mid do(S = s), M) = P_{\text{tr}}(Y \mid S = s). Then

U_f(M) = -\delta(M) < 0,

where

\delta(M) = \mathbb E_{s \sim S^\star} D_{\mathrm{KL}}\bigl(P(Y) \,\|\, P_{\text{tr}}(Y \mid s)\bigr) > 0.

Proof. By Definition 2.4 with X = S and \mathcal T_{cf} randomizing S \sim S^\star,

\mathrm{CE}_{\text{sim}}(M) = \mathbb E_{s \sim S^\star} \mathbb E_{y \sim P(Y)} \bigl[-\log P_{\text{tr}}(y \mid s)\bigr].

Add and subtract H(Y) = \mathbb E_{y \sim P(Y)}[-\log P(Y)]:

\mathrm{CE}_{\text{sim}}(M) = H(Y) + \mathbb E_{s \sim S^\star} D_{\mathrm{KL}}\bigl(P(Y) \,\|\, P_{\text{tr}}(Y \mid s)\bigr).

By Gibbs' inequality (Proposition 1.13), each KL term is non-negative, and strictly positive iff P_{\text{tr}}(Y \mid s) \neq P(Y). Under (A1), the strict inequality holds on a set of S^\star-positive measure. Hence \mathrm{CE}_{\text{sim}}(M) > H(Y), so U_f(M) = H(Y) - \mathrm{CE}_{\text{sim}}(M) = -\delta(M) < 0. \square

Interpretation. The simulator's coding cost for the interventional world exceeds the marginal entropy H(Y) by the average KL divergence between the true interventional marginal and the MLE's conditionals. This quantity \delta(M) is the spurious liability.

3.2 Invariance Criterion (Lemma 2)

Definition 3.2 (Spurious error). For a representation \widetilde M, define

\mathrm{Err}_{\text{spur}}(\widetilde M) = \sup_{Z \subseteq S} \sup_z \bigl\|P_{\text{sim}}(\cdot \mid do(Z=z), \widetilde M) - P_{\text{sim}}(\cdot \mid \widetilde M)\bigr\|_{\mathrm{TV}}.

Lemma 3.3 (Invariance criterion). \mathrm{Err}_{\text{spur}}(\widetilde M) = 0 if and only if P_{\text{sim}}(\cdot \mid do(X), \widetilde M) is \mathcal F_{X_Z^\perp}-measurable.

Proof. (\Rightarrow) If \mathrm{Err}_{\text{spur}}(\widetilde M) = 0, then for every Z \subseteq S and every z,

P_{\text{sim}}(\hat Y \mid do(Z = z), \widetilde M) = P_{\text{sim}}(\hat Y \mid \widetilde M).

By Theorem 1.7, a kernel satisfying this for all Z \subseteq S is \mathcal F_{X_Z^\perp}-measurable.

(\Leftarrow) If P_{\text{sim}} is \mathcal F_{X_Z^\perp}-measurable, it does not depend on Z \subseteq S by definition of the \sigma-algebra, hence \mathrm{Err}_{\text{spur}} = 0. \square

3.3 Non-Identification under Distribution-Preserving Retention (Lemma 3)

Definition 3.4 (Distribution-preserving retention). A subset M_{\text{retain}} \subseteq M is distribution-preserving if

P_{M_{\text{retain}}}(S, Y) = P_M(S, Y),

i.e., the empirical joint distribution over (S, Y) is preserved.

Lemma 3.5 (Non-identification under distribution-preserving retention). Let M_{\text{retain}} \subseteq M be distribution-preserving. Under FEC violation with respect to \mathcal G_{\text{tr}} and under (A1), the maximum-likelihood simulator on M_{\text{retain}} satisfies

\mathrm{Err}_{\text{spur}}(M_{\text{retain}}) = \delta(M) > 0.

Proof. By distribution preservation, P_{M_{\text{retain}}}(Y \mid S = s) = P_M(Y \mid S = s) for all s. By maximum-likelihood estimation,

P_{\text{sim}}(\hat Y \mid do(S = s), M_{\text{retain}}) = P_{M_{\text{retain}}}(Y \mid S = s) = P_M(Y \mid S = s) = P_{\text{tr}}(Y \mid S = s).

FEC violation means there exists h such that

P(Y \mid h(S), g) = P(Y \mid h(S), g') \quad \forall g, g' \in \mathcal G_{\text{tr}},

and the common value differs from P(Y). Since M_{\text{retain}} preserves the distribution over (S, Y), no environment g \in \mathcal G_{\text{tr}} is excluded and no new environment is introduced. Therefore the ML simulator recovers the same conditional P_{\text{tr}}(Y \mid S = s) \neq P(Y), so by Lemma 3.1, \mathrm{Err}_{\text{spur}}(M_{\text{retain}}) = \delta(M) > 0. \square

Remark 3.6 (Degenerate subsets). If M_{\text{retain}} = \{e \in M : S(e) = s_0\}, then S is constant on M_{\text{retain}}, the ML simulator cannot condition on S, and \mathrm{Err}_{\text{spur}} = 0 trivially. Such a subset violates distribution preservation. At matched rate R (Assumption A3), a collapsed-support subset wastes bits on redundancy and is dominated in the retention-vs-distortion comparison by rate arguments.

Remark 3.7 (Information-theoretic core). The lemma is best read as: no subset of M can contain falsifying evidence that M lacked. Deletion removes data; it cannot create the environmental diversity whose absence constitutes FEC violation.

3.4 Geometric Separation of Abstraction and Transformation (Lemma 4)

Definition 3.8 (Feature representation of a quotient). Let A be a quotient-only abstraction with vertex map A: V_M \to V_{A(M)}. For a vertex v \in V_{A(M)}, define \phi(v) \in \mathbb R^d as the feature vector of the cluster A^{-1}(v) \subseteq V_M. The S-invariant projection \phi_{\text{inv}}(v) is the closest point to \phi(v) in the set of S-invariant feature vectors.

Definition 3.9 (Transport scale). Let

\delta_0 = \inf_{v \in V_M : \phi(v) \text{ not } S\text{-invariant}} \|\phi(v) - \phi_{\text{inv}}(v)\|.

This is the minimal distance from a non-S-invariant vertex to the S-invariant subspace.

Lemma 3.10 (Geometric separation). Let A be a quotient-only abstraction and \mathcal D_\theta a non-homomorphic distortion. Under FEC violation, at any distortion level D achieving \mathcal F_{X_Z^\perp}-measurability:

1. Any quotient-only abstraction A that achieves \mathcal F_{X_Z^\perp}-measurability must collapse a vertex carrying both a spurious edge (e_S, e_Y, \mathrm{assoc}) and an invariant edge (e_C, e_Y, \mathrm{causal}).
2. This collapse forces a transport cost of at least

\Delta_{\text{geo}} = (\delta_0 - o(\delta_0))^2 > 0

on the invariant structure, where \delta_0 is the transport scale of Definition 3.9.

3. Non-homomorphic distortion achieves \mathcal F_{X_Z^\perp}-measurability with zero such transport cost.

Proof.

Case 1: A preserves the spurious edge. If (A(e_S), A(e_Y), \mathrm{assoc}) \in E_{A(M)}, then A(M) retains the spurious relation, so by Lemma 3.3, \mathrm{Err}_{\text{spur}}(A(M)) > 0 and A(M) is not \mathcal F_{X_Z^\perp}-measurable. This case is excluded.

Case 2: A collapses the spurious edge. By Definition 2.2, the only way for A to remove the edge (e_S, e_Y, \mathrm{assoc}) while remaining a quotient is to identify A(e_S) = A(e_Y) =: v. Then v is the image of both an S-informative vertex and the Y-vertex.

Measurability of the simulator in \mathcal F_{X_Z^\perp} requires that the representation of v — \phi(v) — be S-invariant. Write \phi_{\text{inv}} for the S-invariant projection. Then

\|\phi(v) - \phi_{\text{inv}}(v)\| \ge \delta_0.

On the other hand, the invariant edge (e_C, e_Y, \mathrm{causal}) must survive the quotient. Since A(e_Y) = v and A(e_C) = A(e_C), the constraint that the causal edge is preserved at cost at most o(\delta_0) forces

\|\phi(v) - \phi(e_Y)\| \le o(\delta_0).

By the triangle inequality on the transport cost c,

c(e_S, e_Y) \le \|\phi(e_S) - \phi(v)\|^2 + \|\phi(v) - \phi(e_Y)\|^2 \le c(e_S, v) + o(\delta_0)^2.

Since \phi(v) must lie at distance \ge \delta_0 from the S-informative region and \phi(e_S) lies in it, we have \|\phi(e_S) - \phi(v)\| \ge \delta_0 - o(\delta_0), hence

c(e_S, v) \ge (\delta_0 - o(\delta_0))^2 > 0.

The transport mass that the quotient forces onto the vertex v is therefore strictly positive, and is borne entirely by the invariant structure incident to v: the causal edge (e_C, e_Y, \mathrm{causal}) must now be encoded through a vertex that has been displaced by at least \delta_0 in feature space. This is the geometric rate penalty:

\Delta_{\text{geo}} = (\delta_0 - o(\delta_0))^2 > 0.

Case 3: \mathcal D_\theta non-homomorphic. By Definition 2.2, \mathcal D_\theta may rewrite the spurious edge

(e_S, e_Y, \mathrm{assoc}) \mapsto (e_S, \varnothing) \quad \text{or} \quad (e_S, e_{Y'}, \mathrm{assoc})

without identifying e_Y with e_S. The invariant edge (e_C, e_Y, \mathrm{causal}) is untouched. No vertex displacement is required, so no \Delta_{\text{geo}} is paid. \square

Assumption (A6) (Rate–transport link). There exists a strictly increasing function \psi: \mathbb R_{\ge 0} \to \mathbb R_{\ge 0} with \psi(0) = 0 such that if a representation class \mathcal C forces a transport cost of at least \Delta_{\text{geo}} on the invariant structure, then

R_c^{\mathcal C}(D) \ge R_c^{\mathrm{PMD}}(D) + \psi(\Delta_{\text{geo}})

for all D \ge D_0.

Corollary 3.11 (Rate penalty for quotient-only abstraction). Under (A6), for quotient-only A,

R_c^{A}(D) \ge R_c^{\mathrm{PMD}}(D) + \Delta, \qquad \Delta = \psi(\Delta_{\text{geo}}) > 0,

with \Delta = 0 only in the boundary case where A is already S-invariant at D = 0.

Proof. Immediate from Lemma 3.10 and (A6). \square

