4. Conditional Existence of Productive Memory Distortion

4.1 Assumptions

(A1) Spurious reliance (Section 3.1).

(A2′) Existence of an invariant distortion with path-measurability (epistemic prior). There exists a non-homomorphic distortion operator \mathcal D_\theta and a level D_0 > 0 such that for all D \in [D_0, D_{\max}]:

· P_{\text{sim}}(\cdot \mid do(X), \mathcal D_D(M)) is \mathcal F_{X_Z^\perp}-measurable;
· the invariant mechanism C \to Y is preserved;
· the map D \mapsto P_{\text{sim}}(\cdot \mid do(X), \mathcal D_D(M)) is continuous in total variation.

This is a prior over which edges are spurious, not a deduction from M.

(A3) Rate constraint. |R_{\text{PMD}}| = |R_{\text{retain}}| = |R_{\text{abstract}}| = R for fixed R, and all retention sets considered are distribution-preserving in the sense of Definition 3.4.

(A4) FEC violation. The training distribution violates the falsity exposure criterion (Zhou et al., Thm 4.2).

(A5a) Local geometry — existence. Define

\epsilon(D) = \mathrm{CE}_{\text{sim}}(\mathcal D_D(M)) - H(Y).

Under (A2′), \epsilon is continuous on [0, D_{\max}]. There exists D^\ast \in [D_0, D_{\max}] with \epsilon(D^\ast) < \delta(M).

(A5b) Local geometry — growth. There exists D_1 \in (0, D_{\max}) with \epsilon(D_1) > \delta(M).

(A6) Rate–transport link (Section 3.4).

4.2 Main Theorem

Theorem 4.1 (Conditional PMD existence). Under (A1), (A2′), (A3), (A4), (A5a), (A6), there exists D^\ast > 0 such that

U_f(\mathcal D_{D^\ast}(M)) > \max\{U_f(M), U_f(M_{\text{retain}}), U_f(A(M))\}

for every distribution-preserving M_{\text{retain}} \subseteq M and every quotient-only abstraction A. Consequently,

\mathrm{FSG}_{\text{sep}}(D^\ast) > 0 \quad \text{and} \quad \mathrm{FSG}_{\text{abs}}(D^\ast) > 0.

4.3 Proof of the Main Theorem

Proof.

Step 1 (Exact memory incurs liability). By Lemma 3.1, under (A1),

U_f(M) = -\delta(M), \qquad \delta(M) > 0.

Step 2 (PMD achieves invariance). By (A2′), for D \in [D_0, D_{\max}], \mathcal D_D(M) is \mathcal F_{X_Z^\perp}-measurable. By Lemma 3.3,

\mathrm{Err}_{\text{spur}}(\mathcal D_D(M)) = 0.

Step 3 (Total error at D^\ast). Define the total excess cross-entropy

\mathrm{Err}(\widetilde M) = \mathrm{CE}_{\text{sim}}(\widetilde M) - H(Y) = -U_f(\widetilde M).

Under (A2′), for D \in [D_0, D_{\max}], the spurious contribution vanishes, so

\mathrm{Err}(\mathcal D_D(M)) = \epsilon(D),

where \epsilon(D) = \mathrm{CE}_{\text{sim}}(\mathcal D_D(M)) - H(Y) is the invariant error. By (A5a), there exists D^\ast \in [D_0, D_{\max}] with

\epsilon(D^\ast) < \delta(M).

Step 4 (PMD dominates exact memory).

\mathrm{Err}(\mathcal D_{D^\ast}(M)) = \epsilon(D^\ast) < \delta(M) = \mathrm{Err}(M),

hence U_f(\mathcal D_{D^\ast}(M)) > U_f(M), so \mathrm{FSG}_{\text{abs}}(D^\ast) > 0.

Step 5 (PMD dominates retention). Let M_{\text{retain}} be any distribution-preserving subsample. By Lemma 3.5,

\mathrm{Err}(M_{\text{retain}}) = \delta(M).

Therefore

\mathrm{Err}(M_{\text{retain}}) = \delta(M) > \epsilon(D^\ast) = \mathrm{Err}(\mathcal D_{D^\ast}(M)),

and hence U_f(\mathcal D_{D^\ast}(M)) > U_f(M_{\text{retain}}).

Step 6 (PMD dominates abstraction). By Corollary 3.11, at equal rate R,

R_c^{A}(D^\ast) \ge R_c^{\mathrm{PMD}}(D^\ast) + \Delta, \qquad \Delta > 0.

Since R_c^{\mathrm{PMD}}(D^\ast) \le R (PMD achieves distortion \epsilon(D^\ast) < \delta(M) at rate R), we have R_c^{A}(D^\ast) > R. Therefore class A cannot achieve distortion \epsilon(D^\ast) at rate R, so

\mathrm{Err}(A(M)) > \epsilon(D^\ast) = \mathrm{Err}(\mathcal D_{D^\ast}(M)).

Hence U_f(\mathcal D_{D^\ast}(M)) > U_f(A(M)).

Conclusion. Combining Steps 4–6,

U_f(\mathcal D_{D^\ast}(M)) > \max\{U_f(M), U_f(M_{\text{retain}}), U_f(A(M))\},

so \mathrm{FSG}_{\text{sep}}(D^\ast) > 0 and \mathrm{FSG}_{\text{abs}}(D^\ast) > 0. \square

