5. Three Corollaries

5.1 The Inverted-U

Corollary 5.1 (Inverted-U). Assume (A2′), (A5a), (A5b). Then U_f(D) has an interior maximum D^\ast \in (0, D_1).

Proof. For D < D_0, \mathrm{Err}_{\text{spur}}(D) > 0 and may dominate. For D \in [D_0, D_{\max}], \mathrm{Err}_{\text{spur}}(D) = 0 and

U_f(D) = -\epsilon(D) + \text{const}.

By (A2′), \epsilon is continuous on [0, D_{\max}]. Since \epsilon(0) = 0 < \delta(M) and \epsilon(D_1) > \delta(M), and \epsilon is continuous, the intermediate value theorem yields D_0' \in (0, D_1) with \epsilon(D_0') = \delta(M). On [0, D_0'], U_f is increasing (spurious reduction dominates); on [D_0', D_1], U_f is decreasing (invariant degradation dominates). By continuity of U_f on compact [0, D_1], an interior maximum exists. \square

5.2 Task Dependence

Definition 5.2 (Interventional volatility).

\mathrm{Vol}(\mathcal T_{cf} \mid P_{\text{tr}}) = \mathbb E_{x \sim \mathcal T_{cf}} D_{\mathrm{KL}}\bigl(P(Y \mid do(X=x)) \,\|\, P_{\text{tr}}(Y \mid X=x)\bigr).

Corollary 5.3 (Task dependence). Assume \delta(M) is monotone non-decreasing in \mathrm{Vol}. At an interior optimum D^\ast in the invariant-dominated regime with second-order condition \partial^2 \mathrm{Err}/\partial D^2 > 0, and assuming the cross-partial \partial^2 \mathrm{Err}_{\text{spur}}/\partial D \partial \mathrm{Vol} < 0,

\frac{\partial D^\ast}{\partial \mathrm{Vol}} > 0.

Proof. Write the total error as

\mathrm{Err}(D, \mathrm{Vol}) = \mathrm{Err}_{\text{spur}}(D, \mathrm{Vol}) + \epsilon(D).

The first-order condition for an interior maximum of U_f = -\mathrm{Err} is

\frac{\partial \mathrm{Err}}{\partial D} = \frac{\partial \mathrm{Err}_{\text{spur}}}{\partial D} + \epsilon'(D) = 0.

Differentiating with respect to \mathrm{Vol}:

\frac{\partial^2 \mathrm{Err}_{\text{spur}}}{\partial D \partial \mathrm{Vol}} + \left(\frac{\partial^2 \mathrm{Err}_{\text{spur}}}{\partial D^2} + \epsilon''(D)\right) \frac{\partial D^\ast}{\partial \mathrm{Vol}} = 0.

Solving,

\frac{\partial D^\ast}{\partial \mathrm{Vol}} = -\frac{\partial^2 \mathrm{Err}_{\text{spur}}/\partial D \partial \mathrm{Vol}}{\partial^2 \mathrm{Err}_{\text{spur}}/\partial D^2 + \epsilon''(D)}.

The denominator is positive by the second-order condition for a maximum (since \mathrm{Err} is convex at the minimum of U_f). The numerator is negative by assumption. Hence \partial D^\ast/\partial \mathrm{Vol} > 0. \square

5.3 The Separation Theorem

Corollary 5.4 (Separation theorem). At equal rate R,

U_f(\mathcal D_\theta(M)) > \max\{U_f(M_{\text{retain}}), U_f(A(M))\}

if and only if FEC is violated and A is quotient-only homomorphic.

Proof.

Sufficiency. Theorem 4.1, Steps 5–6.

Necessity. If FEC holds, Zhou et al. (Thm 4.2) guarantees that retention recovers the invariant predictor, so no transformation advantage exists. If A is non-homomorphic, it coincides with PMD by Definition 2.2, so the comparison is between PMD and itself. \square

Remark 5.5 (Empirical bite). The necessity direction is partly definitional: "if A is non-homomorphic, it coincides with PMD" is true by construction. The theory's empirical content is therefore the claim that real abstraction operators (clustering, summarization, schema compression) behave as quotients under confounding. This is tested by operationalizing A on natural memory representations and measuring R_c^A(D) against R_c^{\mathrm{PMD}}(D) at matched R.

