Appendices

Appendix A: Measure-Theoretic and Probabilistic Foundations

A.1 Polish Spaces, Weak Convergence, and Prokhorov's Theorem

Definition A.1 (Polish space). A Polish space is a separable, completely metrizable topological space. Every compact metric space is Polish. In this work, the episode space \mathcal E is assumed compact metric, hence Polish.

Definition A.2 (Weak convergence). A sequence of probability measures (\mu_n) on a Polish space \mathcal X converges weakly to \mu, written \mu_n \Rightarrow \mu, if

\int f \, d\mu_n \to \int f \, d\mu

for every bounded continuous f: \mathcal X \to \mathbb R.

Definition A.3 (Tightness). A family \mathcal P of probability measures on \mathcal X is tight if for every \varepsilon > 0 there exists a compact K \subseteq \mathcal X with \mu(K) \ge 1 - \varepsilon for all \mu \in \mathcal P.

Theorem A.4 (Prokhorov). Let \mathcal X be Polish and \mathcal P a family of probability measures on \mathcal X. Then \mathcal P is tight if and only if \mathcal P is relatively compact in the weak topology.

Corollary A.5. If \mathcal X is compact, every family of probability measures on \mathcal X is tight, hence relatively compact.

Corollary A.6. If \mathcal X is compact and f: \mathcal X \to \mathbb R is continuous, then f is bounded and \mu \mapsto \int f \, d\mu is continuous in the weak topology.

Proof. Continuous functions on compact spaces are bounded. Weak convergence is defined by convergence of integrals against bounded continuous functions. \square

A.2 Markov Kernels

Definition A.7 (Markov kernel). Let (\mathcal X, \mathcal B_{\mathcal X}) and (\mathcal Y, \mathcal B_{\mathcal Y}) be measurable spaces. A Markov kernel from \mathcal X to \mathcal Y is a map K: \mathcal X \times \mathcal B_{\mathcal Y} \to [0,1] such that:

1. For each B \in \mathcal B_{\mathcal Y}, the map x \mapsto K(x, B) is \mathcal B_{\mathcal X}-measurable.
2. For each x \in \mathcal X, the map B \mapsto K(x, B) is a probability measure on (\mathcal Y, \mathcal B_{\mathcal Y}).

We write K: \mathcal X \rightsquigarrow \mathcal Y.

Definition A.8 (Composition). If K: \mathcal X \rightsquigarrow \mathcal Y and L: \mathcal Y \rightsquigarrow \mathcal Z, the composition LK: \mathcal X \rightsquigarrow \mathcal Z is

(LK)(x, C) = \int_{\mathcal Y} L(y, C) \, K(x, dy).

Associativity holds: (ML)K = M(LK).

Definition A.9 (Pushforward of a kernel). If K: \mathcal X \rightsquigarrow \mathcal Y and f: \mathcal Y \to \mathcal Z is measurable, the pushforward f_\# K: \mathcal X \rightsquigarrow \mathcal Z is

(f_\# K)(x, C) = K(x, f^{-1}(C)).

A.3 Total Variation

Definition A.10 (Total variation). For finite signed measures \mu, \nu on (\mathcal X, \mathcal B_{\mathcal X}),

\|\mu - \nu\|_{\mathrm{TV}} = \sup_{A \in \mathcal B_{\mathcal X}} |\mu(A) - \nu(A)|.

For probability measures, this equals \frac12 \int |d\mu - d\nu|.

Definition A.11 (TV for kernels). For kernels K, L: \mathcal X \rightsquigarrow \mathcal Y,

\|K - L\|_{\mathrm{TV}} = \sup_{x \in \mathcal X} \|K(x, \cdot) - L(x, \cdot)\|_{\mathrm{TV}}.

Proposition A.12 (TV and bounded functions). For any bounded measurable f: \mathcal Y \to \mathbb R with \|f\|_\infty \le 1,

\left| \int f \, dK(x, \cdot) - \int f \, dL(x, \cdot) \right| \le 2 \|K - L\|_{\mathrm{TV}}.

Proof. The difference of integrals equals \int f \, d(K - L). Writing K - L = (K - L)^+ - (K - L)^- and using \|f\|_\infty \le 1,

\left| \int f \, d(K - L) \right| \le \int |f| \, d|K - L| \le \int d|K - L| = 2\|K - L\|_{\mathrm{TV}}. \quad \square

A.4 Conditional Distributions and Disintegration

Theorem A.13 (Disintegration). Let \mu be a probability measure on \mathcal X \times \mathcal Y with marginal \mu_X on \mathcal X. Then there exists a \mu_X-a.e. unique Markov kernel K: \mathcal X \rightsquigarrow \mathcal Y such that

\mu(A \times B) = \int_A K(x, B) \, \mu_X(dx).

We write K(x, B) = \mu(B \mid X = x).

Corollary A.14 (Bayes). Under the setup of Theorem A.13, if \mu = P_{\text{tr}}(C, S, Y), then the conditional P_{\text{tr}}(Y \mid S = s) is well-defined P_{\text{tr}}(S)-a.e.

