Appendix B: Structural Causal Models and Counterfactual Invariance

B.1 Structural Causal Models

Definition B.1 (SCM). A structural causal model (SCM) is a tuple W = (U, V, F) where:

· U = (U_1, \dots, U_k) are exogenous variables, jointly independent.
· V = (V_1, \dots, V_k) are endogenous variables.
· F = (f_1, \dots, f_k) are structural equations V_i = f_i(\mathrm{pa}(V_i), U_i), where \mathrm{pa}(V_i) \subseteq V \cup U \setminus \{V_i\}.

The causal graph G(W) has vertices V \cup U and directed edges from each parent to its child.

Assumption B.2 (Acyclicity). The causal graph G(W) is acyclic. This ensures the structural equations have a unique solution (V_1, \dots, V_k) as a function of U.

Definition B.3 (Observational distribution). The observational distribution P_W is the distribution of (V, U) induced by the exogenous distribution P_U and the structural equations F.

B.2 Interventions

Definition B.4 (Intervention). For Z \subseteq V and a value z, the intervention do(Z = z) replaces the structural equations for Z with constants z. The resulting SCM is W_{do(Z=z)}, and the interventional distribution is P(\cdot \mid do(Z = z)) = P_{W_{do(Z=z)}}.

Definition B.5 (Intervention protocol). An intervention protocol \mathcal T_{cf} is a probability distribution over interventions do(X = x). In this work, X = (X_C, X_S) with X_C acting on C and X_S on S.

Remark B.6 (Modularity). The intervention do(Z = z) modifies only the structural equations for Z. All other equations remain unchanged. This is the modularity assumption of SCMs.

B.3 Counterfactuals

Definition B.7 (Counterfactual). For an intervention do(Z = z), the counterfactual V_i(z) of variable V_i is its value in W_{do(Z=z)} under the same exogenous U. Formally, V_i(z) = f_i(\mathrm{pa}(V_i)(z), U_i) where \mathrm{pa}(V_i)(z) denotes the parents evaluated under do(Z = z).

Definition B.8 (Twin network). The twin network is the SCM containing both the factual variables V and the counterfactual variables V(z), sharing the same exogenous U.

B.4 The Counterfactual-Invariant \sigma-Algebra

Definition B.9 (Counterfactual-invariant \sigma-algebra). For Z \subseteq S and the family of interventions \{do(Z = z)\}_z, define

\mathcal F_{X_Z^\perp} = \sigma\bigl(\{X(z)\}_z\bigr) \cap \sigma(X).

This is the \sigma-algebra of events that are measurable with respect to both the counterfactual action family and the factual action.

Interpretation B.10. An event A \in \mathcal F_{X_Z^\perp} is one whose indicator \mathbf 1_A(X) is invariant under interventions on Z: \mathbf 1_A(X(z)) = \mathbf 1_A(X) for all z. The \sigma-algebra \mathcal F_{X_Z^\perp} captures exactly the information that is robust to the spurious interventions do(Z = z).

B.5 Veitch's Invariance Criterion

Theorem B.11 (Veitch et al., 2021). Let P_{\text{sim}}(\cdot \mid do(X), \widetilde M) be a Markov kernel. Then

P_{\text{sim}}(\hat Y \mid do(Z = z), \widetilde M) = P_{\text{sim}}(\hat Y \mid \widetilde M) \quad \forall Z \subseteq S,\ \forall z

if and only if P_{\text{sim}} is \mathcal F_{X_Z^\perp}-measurable.

Proof sketch. (\Leftarrow) If P_{\text{sim}} is \mathcal F_{X_Z^\perp}-measurable, then P_{\text{sim}}(\hat Y \in B \mid do(X)) is a \mathcal F_{X_Z^\perp}-measurable function of X. By definition of \mathcal F_{X_Z^\perp}, such a function does not depend on Z \subseteq S, hence the equality.

(\Rightarrow) If the kernel is invariant under all do(Z = z), then the function X \mapsto P_{\text{sim}}(\hat Y \in B \mid do(X)) is invariant under all interventions on Z. By the definition of the invariant \sigma-algebra, such a function is \mathcal F_{X_Z^\perp}-measurable. Since this holds for all B, the kernel is \mathcal F_{X_Z^\perp}-measurable. \square

Remark B.12. The theorem is stated for all Z \subseteq S universally. In practice, it suffices to check for the maximal Z = S, since invariance under do(S = s) for all s implies invariance under any Z \subseteq S.

