Appendix C: Optimal Transport and Wasserstein Geometry

C.1 Couplings

Definition C.1 (Coupling). Let \mu, \nu be probability measures on a Polish space (\mathcal E, d). A coupling of (\mu, \nu) is a probability measure \gamma on \mathcal E \times \mathcal E with marginals:

\gamma(A \times \mathcal E) = \mu(A), \qquad \gamma(\mathcal E \times B) = \nu(B)

for all measurable A, B. Write \Gamma(\mu, \nu) for the set of all couplings.

Proposition C.2 (Non-emptiness). \Gamma(\mu, \nu) \neq \varnothing. The product measure \mu \otimes \nu is always a coupling.

Proposition C.3 (Convexity). \Gamma(\mu, \nu) is convex: if \gamma_1, \gamma_2 \in \Gamma(\mu, \nu) and \lambda \in [0,1], then \lambda \gamma_1 + (1-\lambda)\gamma_2 \in \Gamma(\mu, \nu).

C.2 Kantorovich Duality and Existence

Theorem C.4 (Kantorovich duality). Let \mu, \nu be probability measures on a Polish space \mathcal E, and let c: \mathcal E \times \mathcal E \to [0, \infty) be lower semicontinuous. Then

\inf_{\gamma \in \Gamma(\mu, \nu)} \int c \, d\gamma = \sup_{(\phi, \psi) \in \Phi_c} \left\{ \int \phi \, d\mu + \int \psi \, d\nu \right\},

where \Phi_c = \{(\phi, \psi) : \phi, \psi \text{ bounded continuous}, \phi(e) + \psi(\tilde e) \le c(e, \tilde e)\}.

Corollary C.5 (Existence of optimal coupling). If \mathcal E is compact and c is continuous, then the infimum is attained.

Proof. By Corollary A.5, the set of probability measures on \mathcal E \times \mathcal E is compact. The set \Gamma(\mu, \nu) is closed (marginal constraints are continuous under weak convergence) and hence compact. The map \gamma \mapsto \int c \, d\gamma is continuous (since c is continuous on compact \mathcal E \times \mathcal E, hence bounded). A continuous function on a compact set attains its infimum. \square

C.3 Wasserstein Distance

Definition C.6 (Wasserstein distance). For p \ge 1 and cost c(e, \tilde e) = d(e, \tilde e)^p,

W_p(\mu, \nu) = \left( \inf_{\gamma \in \Gamma(\mu, \nu)} \int d(e, \tilde e)^p \, d\gamma \right)^{1/p}.

Proposition C.7 (Metric properties). W_p is a metric on the set of probability measures with finite p-th moment.

Proof.

· Symmetry: d(e, \tilde e) = d(\tilde e, e) implies symmetry of the cost, hence of the infimum.
· Triangle inequality: the gluing lemma (see Villani, Optimal Transport, Ch. 6) gives W_p(\mu, \nu) \le W_p(\mu, \rho) + W_p(\rho, \nu).
· Identity: W_p(\mu, \nu) = 0 iff \mu = \nu. If \mu = \nu, the diagonal coupling \gamma(A \times B) = \mu(A \cap B) gives cost 0. Conversely, if W_p(\mu, \nu) = 0, then there exists a coupling supported on the diagonal, which forces \mu = \nu. \square

Definition C.8 (Feature-based Wasserstein). For \phi: \mathcal E \to \mathbb R^d and cost c(e, \tilde e) = \|\phi(e) - \phi(\tilde e)\|^2,

W_c(\mu, \nu) = \inf_{\gamma \in \Gamma(\mu, \nu)} \int \|\phi(e) - \phi(\tilde e)\|^2 \, d\gamma.

This is the squared Wasserstein-2 distance between \phi_\# \mu and \phi_\# \nu.

C.4 Pushforwards and Injectivity

Proposition C.9 (Pushforward identity). W_c(\mu, \nu) = W_2^2(\phi_\# \mu, \phi_\# \nu).

Proof. For any coupling \gamma \in \Gamma(\mu, \nu), the pushforward (\phi \times \phi)_\# \gamma is a coupling of \phi_\# \mu and \phi_\# \nu, and

\int \|\phi(e) - \phi(\tilde e)\|^2 \, d\gamma = \int \|u - v\|^2 \, d(\phi \times \phi)_\# \gamma.

Conversely, any coupling of \phi_\# \mu and \phi_\# \nu can be lifted to a coupling of \mu, \nu (by the measurable selection theorem). Hence the infima coincide. \square

Proposition C.10 (Injectivity). If \phi is injective, then W_c(\mu, \nu) = 0 \iff \mu = \nu. If \phi is not injective, then W_c(\mu, \nu) = 0 \iff \phi_\# \mu = \phi_\# \nu.

Proof. By Proposition C.9 and Proposition C.7, W_c(\mu, \nu) = 0 \iff \phi_\# \mu = \phi_\# \nu. If \phi is injective, \phi_\# \mu = \phi_\# \nu implies \mu = \nu (since \phi^{-1} is well-defined on the image). \square

C.5 Triangle Inequality on Transport Cost

Lemma C.11 (Triangle inequality). For any e, \tilde e, \bar e \in \mathcal E,

\|\phi(e) - \phi(\bar e)\| \le \|\phi(e) - \phi(\tilde e)\| + \|\phi(\tilde e) - \phi(\bar e)\|.

Proof. Immediate from the Euclidean triangle inequality applied to \phi(e), \phi(\tilde e), \phi(\bar e) \in \mathbb R^d. \square

Corollary C.12. For squared costs,

c(e, \bar e) \le 2c(e, \tilde e) + 2c(\tilde e, \bar e).

Proof. Square both sides of Lemma C.11 and use (a+b)^2 \le 2a^2 + 2b^2. \square

