Appendix D: Information-Theoretic Foundations

D.1 Entropy, Cross-Entropy, KL Divergence

Definition D.1 (Entropy). For a probability distribution P on a finite set \mathcal Y,

H(P) = -\sum_{y \in \mathcal Y} P(y) \log P(y),

with the convention 0 \log 0 = 0. All logarithms are natural unless stated otherwise.

Definition D.2 (Cross-entropy). For probability distributions P, Q on \mathcal Y,

\mathrm{CE}(P, Q) = -\sum_y P(y) \log Q(y),

with the convention that \mathrm{CE}(P, Q) = +\infty if P(y) > 0 and Q(y) = 0 for some y.

Definition D.3 (KL divergence). For probability distributions P, Q on \mathcal Y,

D_{\mathrm{KL}}(P \| Q) = \sum_y P(y) \log \frac{P(y)}{Q(y)},

with D_{\mathrm{KL}}(P \| Q) = +\infty if P is not absolutely continuous with respect to Q.

Proposition D.4 (Decomposition). \mathrm{CE}(P, Q) = H(P) + D_{\mathrm{KL}}(P \| Q).

Proof.

\mathrm{CE}(P, Q) = -\sum_y P(y) \log Q(y) = -\sum_y P(y) \log P(y) + \sum_y P(y) \log \frac{P(y)}{Q(y)}. \quad \square

D.2 Gibbs' Inequality

Theorem D.5 (Gibbs). For probability distributions P, Q on \mathcal Y,

D_{\mathrm{KL}}(P \| Q) \ge 0,

with equality iff P = Q.

Proof. Since -\log is convex, Jensen's inequality gives

D_{\mathrm{KL}}(P \| Q) = \sum_y P(y) \left( -\log \frac{Q(y)}{P(y)} \right) \ge -\log \sum_y P(y) \frac{Q(y)}{P(y)} = -\log \sum_y Q(y) = 0.

Equality in Jensen holds iff Q(y)/P(y) is constant P-a.e., which with normalization gives P = Q. \square

Corollary D.6. \mathrm{CE}(P, Q) \ge H(P) with equality iff P = Q.

D.3 Continuity of Cross-Entropy under TV

Proposition D.7. Let P be a probability distribution on a finite set \mathcal Y with P(y) > 0 for all y. Let Q, Q' be probability distributions on \mathcal Y with Q'(y) \ge \alpha > 0 for all y. Then

|\mathrm{CE}(P, Q) - \mathrm{CE}(P, Q')| \le \frac{1}{\alpha} \|Q - Q'\|_1 \le \frac{2}{\alpha} \|Q - Q'\|_{\mathrm{TV}}.

Proof. For each y,

|\log Q(y) - \log Q'(y)| = \left| \log \frac{Q(y)}{Q'(y)} \right| = \left| \log \left( 1 + \frac{Q(y) - Q'(y)}{Q'(y)} \right) \right|.

Using |\log(1 + t)| \le |t| / (1 - |t|) for |t| < 1 and Q'(y) \ge \alpha, we get |\log Q(y) - \log Q'(y)| \le |Q(y) - Q'(y)| / \alpha. Multiplying by P(y) and summing:

|\mathrm{CE}(P, Q) - \mathrm{CE}(P, Q')| \le \sum_y P(y) \frac{|Q(y) - Q'(y)|}{\alpha} \le \frac{1}{\alpha} \|Q - Q'\|_1. \quad \square

Corollary D.8. If Q_n \to Q in total variation and Q is bounded below by \alpha > 0, then \mathrm{CE}(P, Q_n) \to \mathrm{CE}(P, Q).

D.4 Data Processing Inequality

Theorem D.9 (Data processing). If X \to Y \to Z is a Markov chain, then

I(X; Z) \le I(X; Y).

Proof. Standard; see Cover & Thomas, Elements of Information Theory, Ch. 2. \square

