Appendix E: Proofs of the Four Lemmas

E.1 Lemma 1: Spurious Liability

Lemma E.1 (Spurious liability). Under (A1), let P_{\text{sim}} be the maximum-likelihood simulator on M, so that P_{\text{sim}}(\hat Y \mid do(S = s), M) = P_{\text{tr}}(Y \mid S = s). Then

U_f(M) = -\delta(M) < 0,

where

\delta(M) = \mathbb E_{s \sim S^\star} D_{\mathrm{KL}}\bigl(P(Y) \,\|\, P_{\text{tr}}(Y \mid s)\bigr) > 0.

Proof. By Definition 2.4 with X = S and \mathcal T_{cf} randomizing S \sim S^\star,

\mathrm{CE}_{\text{sim}}(M) = \mathbb E_{s \sim S^\star} \mathbb E_{y \sim P(Y)} \bigl[-\log P_{\text{tr}}(y \mid s)\bigr].

Write H(Y) = \mathbb E_{y \sim P(Y)}[-\log P(Y)]. Then

\mathrm{CE}_{\text{sim}}(M) - H(Y) = \mathbb E_{s \sim S^\star} \mathbb E_{y \sim P(Y)} \left[ \log \frac{P(Y)}{P_{\text{tr}}(Y \mid s)} \right] = \mathbb E_{s \sim S^\star} D_{\mathrm{KL}}\bigl(P(Y) \,\|\, P_{\text{tr}}(Y \mid s)\bigr).

By Gibbs' inequality (Theorem D.5), each KL term is non-negative, and strictly positive iff P_{\text{tr}}(Y \mid s) \neq P(Y). Under (A1), the strict inequality holds on a set of S^\star-positive measure, so the expectation is strictly positive. Hence \mathrm{CE}_{\text{sim}}(M) > H(Y), so U_f(M) = H(Y) - \mathrm{CE}_{\text{sim}}(M) = -\delta(M) < 0. \square

Remark E.2. The cross-entropy formulation is essential. If one instead used \mathbb E_{s \sim S^\star}[H(P_{\text{tr}}(Y \mid s))], the inequality \mathbb E_{s \sim S^\star}[H(P_{\text{tr}}(Y \mid s))] > H(Y) would fail when S^\star = P_{\text{tr}}(S), since conditioning reduces entropy on average. The cross-entropy formulation avoids this by measuring the coding cost of the interventional marginal under the MLE's conditionals.

E.2 Lemma 2: Invariance Criterion

Lemma E.3 (Invariance criterion). \mathrm{Err}_{\text{spur}}(\widetilde M) = 0 if and only if P_{\text{sim}}(\cdot \mid do(X), \widetilde M) is \mathcal F_{X_Z^\perp}-measurable.

Proof.

(\Rightarrow) Suppose \mathrm{Err}_{\text{spur}}(\widetilde M) = 0. Then for every Z \subseteq S and every z,

P_{\text{sim}}(\hat Y \mid do(Z = z), \widetilde M) = P_{\text{sim}}(\hat Y \mid \widetilde M).

By Theorem B.11, a kernel satisfying this for all Z \subseteq S is \mathcal F_{X_Z^\perp}-measurable.

(\Leftarrow) Suppose P_{\text{sim}} is \mathcal F_{X_Z^\perp}-measurable. By definition of \mathcal F_{X_Z^\perp}, the kernel does not depend on Z \subseteq S. Hence for all Z \subseteq S and all z,

P_{\text{sim}}(\hat Y \mid do(Z = z), \widetilde M) = P_{\text{sim}}(\hat Y \mid \widetilde M),

so \mathrm{Err}_{\text{spur}} = 0. \square

E.3 Lemma 3: Non-Identification under Distribution-Preserving Retention

Definition E.4 (Distribution-preserving retention). A subset M_{\text{retain}} \subseteq M is distribution-preserving if

P_{M_{\text{retain}}}(S, Y) = P_M(S, Y).

Lemma E.5 (Non-identification). Let M_{\text{retain}} \subseteq M be distribution-preserving. Under FEC violation with respect to \mathcal G_{\text{tr}} and under (A1), the maximum-likelihood simulator on M_{\text{retain}} satisfies

\mathrm{Err}_{\text{spur}}(M_{\text{retain}}) = \delta(M) > 0.

Proof. By distribution preservation, P_{M_{\text{retain}}}(Y \mid S = s) = P_M(Y \mid S = s) for all s. By maximum-likelihood estimation,

P_{\text{sim}}(\hat Y \mid do(S = s), M_{\text{retain}}) = P_{M_{\text{retain}}}(Y \mid S = s) = P_M(Y \mid S = s) = P_{\text{tr}}(Y \mid S = s).

FEC violation means there exists h such that

P(Y \mid h(S), g) = P(Y \mid h(S), g') \quad \forall g, g' \in \mathcal G_{\text{tr}},

and the common value differs from P(Y). Since M_{\text{retain}} preserves the distribution over (S, Y), no environment g \in \mathcal G_{\text{tr}} is excluded and no new environment is introduced. Therefore the ML simulator recovers the same conditional P_{\text{tr}}(Y \mid S = s) \neq P(Y). By the same calculation as in Lemma E.1,

\mathrm{CE}_{\text{sim}}(M_{\text{retain}}) - H(Y) = \mathbb E_{s \sim S^\star} D_{\mathrm{KL}}\bigl(P(Y) \,\|\, P_{\text{tr}}(Y \mid s)\bigr) = \delta(M).

Hence \mathrm{Err}_{\text{spur}}(M_{\text{retain}}) = \delta(M) > 0. \square

Remark E.6 (Degenerate subsets). If M_{\text{retain}} = \{e \in M : S(e) = s_0\}, then S is constant on M_{\text{retain}}, the ML simulator cannot condition on S, and \mathrm{Err}_{\text{spur}} = 0 trivially. Such a subset violates distribution preservation. At matched rate R (Assumption A3), a collapsed-support subset wastes bits on redundancy and is dominated in the retention-vs-distortion comparison by rate arguments.

Remark E.7 (Information-theoretic core). The lemma is best read as: no subset of M can contain falsifying evidence that M lacked. Deletion removes data; it cannot create the environmental diversity whose absence constitutes FEC violation.

E.4 Lemma 4: Geometric Separation of Abstraction and Transformation

Definition E.8 (Feature representation of a quotient). Let A be a quotient-only abstraction with vertex map A: V_M \to V_{A(M)}. For a vertex v \in V_{A(M)}, define \phi(v) \in \mathbb R^d as the feature vector of the cluster A^{-1}(v) \subseteq V_M. The S-invariant projection \phi_{\text{inv}}(v) is the closest point to \phi(v) in the set of S-invariant feature vectors.

Definition E.9 (Transport scale). Let

\delta_0 = \inf_{v \in V_M : \phi(v) \text{ not } S\text{-invariant}} \|\phi(v) - \phi_{\text{inv}}(v)\|.

This is the minimal distance from a non-S-invariant vertex to the S-invariant subspace.

Lemma E.10 (Geometric separation). Let A be a quotient-only abstraction and \mathcal D_\theta a non-homomorphic distortion. Under FEC violation, at any distortion level D achieving \mathcal F_{X_Z^\perp}-measurability:

1. Any quotient-only abstraction A that achieves \mathcal F_{X_Z^\perp}-measurability must collapse a vertex carrying both a spurious edge (e_S, e_Y, \mathrm{assoc}) and an invariant edge (e_C, e_Y, \mathrm{causal}).
2. This collapse forces a transport cost of at least

\Delta_{\text{geo}} = (\delta_0 - o(\delta_0))^2 > 0

on the invariant structure.

3. Non-homomorphic distortion achieves \mathcal F_{X_Z^\perp}-measurability with zero such transport cost.

Proof.

Case 1: A preserves the spurious edge. If (A(e_S), A(e_Y), \mathrm{assoc}) \in E_{A(M)}, then A(M) retains the spurious relation, so by Lemma E.3, \mathrm{Err}_{\text{spur}}(A(M)) > 0 and A(M) is not \mathcal F_{X_Z^\perp}-measurable. This case is excluded.

Case 2: A collapses the spurious edge. By Definition 2.2, the only way for A to remove the edge (e_S, e_Y, \mathrm{assoc}) while remaining a quotient is to identify A(e_S) = A(e_Y) =: v. Then v is the image of both an S-informative vertex and the Y-vertex.

Measurability of the simulator in \mathcal F_{X_Z^\perp} requires that the representation of v — \phi(v) — be S-invariant. Write \phi_{\text{inv}} for the S-invariant projection. Then

\|\phi(v) - \phi_{\text{inv}}(v)\| \ge \delta_0.

On the other hand, the invariant edge (e_C, e_Y, \mathrm{causal}) must survive the quotient. Since A(e_Y) = v and A(e_C) = A(e_C), the constraint that the causal edge is preserved at cost at most o(\delta_0) forces

\|\phi(v) - \phi(e_Y)\| \le o(\delta_0).

By the triangle inequality on the transport cost c,

c(e_S, e_Y) \le \|\phi(e_S) - \phi(v)\|^2 + \|\phi(v) - \phi(e_Y)\|^2 \le c(e_S, v) + o(\delta_0)^2.

Since \phi(v) must lie at distance \ge \delta_0 from the S-informative region and \phi(e_S) lies in it, we have \|\phi(e_S) - \phi(v)\| \ge \delta_0 - o(\delta_0), hence

c(e_S, v) \ge (\delta_0 - o(\delta_0))^2 > 0.

The transport mass that the quotient forces onto the vertex v is therefore strictly positive, and is borne entirely by the invariant structure incident to v: the causal edge (e_C, e_Y, \mathrm{causal}) must now be encoded through a vertex that has been displaced by at least \delta_0 in feature space. This is the geometric rate penalty:

\Delta_{\text{geo}} = (\delta_0 - o(\delta_0))^2 > 0.

Case 3: \mathcal D_\theta non-homomorphic. By Definition 2.2, \mathcal D_\theta may rewrite the spurious edge

(e_S, e_Y, \mathrm{assoc}) \mapsto (e_S, \varnothing) \quad \text{or} \quad (e_S, e_{Y'}, \mathrm{assoc})

without identifying e_Y with e_S. The invariant edge (e_C, e_Y, \mathrm{causal}) is untouched. No vertex displacement is required, so no \Delta_{\text{geo}} is paid. \square

Assumption E.11 (Rate–transport link). There exists a strictly increasing function \psi: \mathbb R_{\ge 0} \to \mathbb R_{\ge 0} with \psi(0) = 0 such that if a representation class \mathcal C forces a transport cost of at least \Delta_{\text{geo}} on the invariant structure, then

R_c^{\mathcal C}(D) \ge R_c^{\mathrm{PMD}}(D) + \psi(\Delta_{\text{geo}})

for all D \ge D_0.

Corollary E.12 (Rate penalty for quotient-only abstraction). Under Assumption E.11, for quotient-only A,

R_c^{A}(D) \ge R_c^{\mathrm{PMD}}(D) + \Delta, \qquad \Delta = \psi(\Delta_{\text{geo}}) > 0,

with \Delta = 0 only in the boundary case where A is already S-invariant at D = 0.

Proof. Immediate from Lemma E.10 and Assumption E.11. \square

