Appendix H: Rate-Distortion Theory Details

H.1 Rate-Distortion Function

Definition H.1 (Rate-distortion function). Let \mathcal C be a class of representations, and let \mathrm{Err}: \mathcal C \to \mathbb R_{\ge 0} be a distortion functional. For D \ge 0,

R_c^{\mathcal C}(D) = \inf\{|R| : \widetilde M \in \mathcal C,\ \mathrm{Err}(\widetilde M) \le D,\ P_{\text{sim}}(\cdot \mid do(X), \widetilde M) \text{ is } \mathcal F_{X_Z^\perp}\text{-measurable}\}.

Proposition H.2 (Monotonicity). R_c^{\mathcal C}(D) is non-increasing in D.

Proof. The feasible set for R_c^{\mathcal C}(D) is nested increasing in D: if D_1 \le D_2, then \{\widetilde M : \mathrm{Err}(\widetilde M) \le D_1\} \subseteq \{\widetilde M : \mathrm{Err}(\widetilde M) \le D_2\}. Taking infima gives R_c^{\mathcal C}(D_1) \ge R_c^{\mathcal C}(D_2). \square

Proposition H.3 (Class inclusion). If \mathcal C_1 \subseteq \mathcal C_2, then R_c^{\mathcal C_1}(D) \ge R_c^{\mathcal C_2}(D).

Proof. The feasible set for \mathcal C_1 is contained in that for \mathcal C_2, so the infimum over the smaller set is at least as large. \square

H.2 Convexity

Proposition H.4 (Convexity under mixing). If \mathcal C is closed under convex combinations of representations and \mathrm{Err} is convex, then R_c^{\mathcal C}(D) is convex in D.

Proof. Standard; see Cover & Thomas, Ch. 10. \square

H.3 The Rate–Transport Link

Assumption H.5 (Rate–transport link). There exists a strictly increasing function \psi: \mathbb R_{\ge 0} \to \mathbb R_{\ge 0} with \psi(0) = 0 such that if a representation class \mathcal C forces a transport cost of at least \Delta_{\text{geo}} on the invariant structure, then

R_c^{\mathcal C}(D) \ge R_c^{\mathrm{PMD}}(D) + \psi(\Delta_{\text{geo}})

for all D \ge D_0.

Remark H.6. Assumption H.5 is a substantive claim about the geometry of representation classes. It says that transport cost on the invariant structure cannot be "hidden" by rate-efficient coding; the invariant structure must be encoded explicitly, and any displacement of it costs bits. This is the rate-theoretic content of the separation theorem.

Remark H.7 (Falsifiability). Assumption H.5 is falsifiable: if one exhibits a quotient-only abstraction that achieves the same rate-distortion profile as PMD despite paying \Delta_{\text{geo}} > 0, the assumption is refuted.

