Appendix I: Identifiability and FEC

I.1 The Falsity Exposure Criterion

Definition I.1 (Falsity exposure criterion). A training distribution P_{\text{tr}} with environments \mathcal G_{\text{tr}} satisfies the falsity exposure criterion (FEC) if for every pair of environments g, g' \in \mathcal G_{\text{tr}} and every function h of S,

P(Y \mid h(S), g) \neq P(Y \mid h(S), g') \quad \text{unless } h(S) \text{ is } C\text{-measurable}.

Interpretation I.2. FEC says that the training environments are diverse enough to expose any spurious dependence. If a predictor relies on S, there exist two environments where the conditional P(Y \mid h(S)) differs, providing falsifying evidence.

I.2 Zhou et al. Theorem

Theorem I.3 (Zhou et al., Thm 4.2). If FEC holds, then the invariant predictor P(Y \mid C) is identifiable from P_{\text{tr}}: any predictor that matches P_{\text{tr}} across all environments g \in \mathcal G_{\text{tr}} must equal P(Y \mid C).

Corollary I.4 (FEC violation and non-identification). If FEC is violated, there exists a function h of S such that

P(Y \mid h(S), g) = P(Y \mid h(S), g') \quad \forall g, g' \in \mathcal G_{\text{tr}},

and the common value differs from P(Y). Hence no algorithm based solely on P_{\text{tr}} can distinguish the spurious predictor P(Y \mid h(S)) from the invariant predictor P(Y \mid C).

I.3 Non-Identification under Retention

Lemma I.5. Let M_{\text{retain}} \subseteq M be distribution-preserving. Under FEC violation and (A1), the maximum-likelihood simulator on M_{\text{retain}} has

\mathrm{Err}_{\text{spur}}(M_{\text{retain}}) = \delta(M) > 0.

Proof. This is Lemma E.5 restated. The key point is that distribution preservation means the retention set carries the same (S, Y)-distribution as M, so the ML conditional is unchanged. FEC violation means the stable conditional P(Y \mid h(S)) is not P(Y \mid C), so the spurious error persists. \square

Remark I.6. The non-identification is information-theoretic: no subset of M can contain falsifying evidence that M lacked. Deletion removes data; it cannot create the environmental diversity whose absence constitutes FEC violation.

